apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/aggregate.ts re-implements in TypeScript the
modified mean-field theory as optool's optool_fractal.f90 (R. Tazaki's OPACFRACTAL v3.0) builds it,
for its -mmf option: the translation coefficients and the mean-field system, the geometric-optics
absorption, and Tazaki 2021's geometric cross-section, with its own numerical methods. Release
1.9.14, commit 21aecb46, fetched 2026-10-10.

================================================================================
Data

Values derived from the following data sets are committed as constants or reduced tables with
their citations. The data sets themselves are not committed, except the CIE's, which are kept
unmodified in a tool path (the CIE entry above) (plan R05, R05.T12.d, ruled 2026-10-02; plan R08,
ruled 2026-10-09 and 2026-10-10).

- Solar spectral irradiance values derived from ASTM E490-00a(2019), obtained from the U.S. DOE
  National Laboratory of the Rockies (NLR, formerly NREL); credit DOE/NLR/Alliance. In
  apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/solar.ts.

- CIE 2019, Colour-matching functions of CIE 1931 standard colorimetric observer, International
  Commission on Illumination (CIE), Vienna, AT, DOI: 10.25039/CIE.DS.xvudnb9b
  (https://cie.co.at/datatable/cie-1931-colour-matching-functions-2-degree-observer), licensed
  under CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/).
  - apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/colourMatching.json is adapted material: the
    CIE's table, 360-830 nm at 1 nm, with x_bar, y_bar and z_bar taken to linear Rec. 709 r, g
    and b by the matrix the file records (apps/hyperion/scripts/atmosphereData.mjs matching; plan
    R08, R08.T4.a). It ships in the app and is offered under CC BY-SA 4.0.
  - The values in apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/solar.ts are computed from the
    CIE's table, integrated against E-490 (plan R05). The fitted channel wavelengths in
    apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/channels.json (R08.T4.a) and the test record
    apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/absorbers/curves.json (R08.T4.b) are computed
    with colourMatching.json. To the extent these are adapted material they are offered under
    CC BY-SA 4.0.

- Ozone absorption cross-sections: Serdyuchenko et al., Atmos. Meas. Tech. 7 (2014) 625;
  Gorshelev et al., Atmos. Meas. Tech. 7 (2014) 609 (IUP Bremen, file
  serdyuchenkogorshelev5digits.dat, fetched 2026-10-10). At 233 K, as binned by Bruneton 2017, in
  apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/earth.ts; at 193-293 K, binned to 1 nm, in
  apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/absorbers/crossSections.json.

- Earth's ozone profile: the U.S. Standard model's ozone mixing ratio by pressure, 0-120 km, from
  G. P. Anderson, S. A. Clough, F. X. Kneizys, J. H. Chetwynd and E. P. Shettle, "AFGL
  Atmospheric Constituent Profiles (0-120 km)", AFGL-TR-86-0110, Environmental Research Papers
  No. 954, Air Force Geophysics Laboratory, Hanscom AFB, 15 May 1986, Table 1f (DTIC ADA175173;
  approved for public release, distribution unlimited; a work of the U.S. Government), which
  follows the U.S. Standard Atmosphere 1976's mid-latitude ozone model (A. J. Krueger and R. A.
  Minzner, J. Geophys. Res. 81(24) (1976) 4477-4481) to 2% from 2 to 50 km. Read from the
  report, not taken from a redistribution, and committed reduced (pressure and mixing ratio) in
  apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/earth.ts (plan R08, R08.T6.h).

- Methane absorption cross-sections at 100, 198 and 296 K: E. Karkoschka and M. G. Tomasko,
  "Methane absorption coefficients for the jovian planets from laboratory, Huygens, and HST
  data", Icarus 205 (2010) 674-694, DOI: 10.1016/j.icarus.2009.07.044, as converted to
  cross-sections by NASA's Planetary Spectrum Generator (G. L. Villanueva et al., J. Quant.
  Spectrosc. Radiat. Transfer 217 (2018) 86-104; https://psg.gsfc.nasa.gov, file
  data/linelists/xuv/data/ch4.txt, fetched 2026-10-09). Resampled values in
  apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/absorbers/crossSections.json.

- Sulphur dioxide absorption cross-sections at 298, 318, 338 and 358 K: C. Hermans, A. C.
  Vandaele and S. Fally, J. Quant. Spectrosc. Radiat. Transfer 110 (2009) 756-765, DOI:
  10.1016/j.jqsrt.2009.01.031; A. C. Vandaele, C. Hermans and S. Fally, J. Quant. Spectrosc.
  Radiat. Transfer 110 (2009) 2115-2126, DOI: 10.1016/j.jqsrt.2009.05.006; as held in the
  MPI-Mainz UV/VIS Spectral Atlas of Gaseous Molecules' snapshot of 2013-06-24 (H. Keller-Rudek,
  G. K. Moortgat, R. Sander and R. Sorensen, Earth Syst. Sci. Data 5 (2013) 365-373, DOI:
  10.5194/essd-5-365-2013; Zenodo, DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.6951), licensed under CC BY 4.0
  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/); fetched 2026-10-10. Binned to 1 nm, a
  modification, in apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/absorbers/crossSections.json.

- Collision-induced absorption of O2-O2, O2-N2, O2-CO2 and H2-He, from the HITRAN2024 database's
  collision-induced absorption section (I. E. Gordon et al., J. Quant. Spectrosc. Radiat.
  Transfer 353 (2026) 109807, DOI: 10.1016/j.jqsrt.2026.109807; J. Terragni et al., J. Quant.
  Spectrosc. Radiat. Transfer 347 (2025) 109631, DOI: 10.1016/j.jqsrt.2025.109631; T. Karman et
  al., Icarus 328 (2019) 160-175, DOI: 10.1016/j.icarus.2019.02.034; https://hitran.org/cia/,
  files O2-O2_2024.cia, O2-N2_2024.cia, O2-CO2_2024.cia and H2-He_2011.cia, fetched 2026-10-10
  and 2026-10-11, and the alternate files O2-O2_theory_temp_2018.cia and
  O2-N2_theory_temp_2018.cia, fetched 2026-10-12, the computed A- and B-band sets of T. Karman et
  al., Nature Chem. 10 (2018) 549-554, DOI: 10.1038/s41557-018-0015-x), from the measurements and
  calculations of H. Finkenzeller and R. Volkamer, J.
  Quant. Spectrosc. Radiat. Transfer 279 (2022) 108063; R. Thalman and R. Volkamer, Phys. Chem.
  Chem. Phys. 15 (2013) 15371; F. R. Spiering et al., Phys. Chem. Chem. Phys. 13 (2011) 9616; H.
  Tran, C. Boulet and J.-M. Hartmann, J. Geophys. Res. 111 (2006) D15210; M. Vangvichith, H. Tran
  and J.-M. Hartmann, J. Quant. Spectrosc. Radiat. Transfer 110 (2009) 2212-2216, DOI:
  10.1016/j.jqsrt.2009.06.002; and M. Abel, L. Frommhold, X. Li and K. L. C. Hunt, J. Chem. Phys.
  136 (2012) 044319. Binned to 1 nm in
  apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/absorbers/crossSections.json (H2-He) and
  apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/absorbers/oxygenPairs.json (O2's pairs; plan R08,
  R08.T4.d).

- The negative hydrogen and helium ions' continuous absorption: T. L. John, "Continuous absorption
  by the negative hydrogen ion reconsidered", Astron. Astrophys. 193 (1988) 189-192, his fits'
  coefficients (Eqs. 3-6, Tables 2, 3a and 3b), in
  apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/absorbers/hMinus.ts; and T. L. John, "Absorption
  coefficients of the negative helium ion", Mon. Not. R. Astron. Soc. 269 (1994) 871-878, his
  Table 2's free-free values over 506-911 nm, in
  apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/absorbers/heliumMinus.ts (plan R08, R08.T4.c).

- Atomic line data: R. L. Kurucz, atomic line lists, http://kurucz.harvard.edu/linelists/gfall/
  (the files named in each value's source, fetched 2026-10-11; R. L. Kurucz, "Including all the
  lines", Can. J. Phys. 89 (2011) 417). The bounds of the atoms and ions in
  apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/absorbers/bounds.json are computed from them (plan
  R08, R08.T4.c), with P. S. Barklem and R. Collet's partition functions, Astron. Astrophys. 588
  (2016) A96, via CDS VizieR (DOI: 10.26093/cds/vizier), catalogue J/A+A/588/A96. The forbidden
  [Fe II] lines Kurucz lacks are N. C. Deb and A. Hibbert's, "Radiative transition rates for the
  forbidden lines in Fe II", Astron. Astrophys. 536 (2011) A74, DOI: 10.1051/0004-6361/201118059,
  via CDS VizieR, catalogue J/A+A/536/A74 (fetched 2026-10-11); the forbidden
  [Ti II] lines' are NIST's Atomic Spectra Database v5.12 values (A. Kramida, Yu. Ralchenko, J.
  Reader and NIST ASD Team, 2024, DOI: 10.18434/T4W30F; a stated exception, Kurucz lacking them
  and their primary papers being closed), which ASD attributes to N. C. Deb, A. Hibbert, Z.
  Felfli and A. Z. Msezane, J. Phys. B 42 (2009) 015701, and H. Hartman et al., Mon. Not. R.
  Astron. Soc. 361 (2005) 206.

- H2-H2 collision-induced absorption: A. Borysow, "Collision-induced absorption coefficients of H2
  pairs at temperatures from 60 K to 1000 K", Astron. Astrophys. 390 (2002) 779-782, DOI
  10.1051/0004-6361:20020555, from the tables at http://www.astro.ku.dk/~aborysow/programs/
  (fetched 2026-10-11). Above 1000 K: A. Borysow, U. G. Jorgensen and Y. Fu, J. Quant. Spectrosc.
  Radiat. Transfer 68 (2001) 235-255, from the same page. Reduced to 1 nm cell means in
  apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/absorbers/crossSections.json (plan R08,
  R08.T4.c); the tables themselves are not committed.

- Water vapour continuum: the Clough-Kneizys-Davies continuum as tabulated in LOWTRAN 7
  (F. X. Kneizys et al., "Users Guide to LOWTRAN 7", AFGL-TR-88-0177, Air Force Geophysics
  Laboratory, 1988; formulation in "Atmospheric Transmittance/Radiance: Computer Code LOWTRAN 6",
  AFGL-TR-83-0187, 1983), from lowtran7.f at https://github.com/space-physics/lowtran (fetched
  2026-10-12). Reduced to 1 nm cell means in
  apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/absorbers/crossSections.json (plan R08,
  R08.T4.d); the tables themselves are not committed.

- Molecular lines' k-distributions, reduced from the HITRAN2024 line lists (I. E. Gordon et al.,
  as above; https://hitran.org/lbl/, fetched 2026-10-12) of O2, H2O, CO2, CO, HCN and H2, and
  from the anchor bands of the stated overtone-ladder estimates of C2H2, C2H4, C2H6, CH3OH and PH3,
  with HITRAN's partition sums (TIPS; R. R. Gamache et al., J. Quant. Spectrosc. Radiat. Transfer
  271 (2021) 107713, DOI: 10.1016/j.jqsrt.2021.107713), to 16-bit k-distributions per 1 nm cell in
  apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/absorbers/kDistributions/ (plan R08, R08.T4.d);
  the line lists themselves are not committed. HITRAN2024's four N2O bands (fetched 2026-10-12 and
  2026-10-13) enter N2O's merged table with ExoMol's TYM list, in
  apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/absorbers/exomol/kDistributions/ (plan R08,
  R08.T4.g; the ExoMol entry below). CO2's far wings take the CO2-CO2 and CO2-N2 chi
  factors and line shape of SpeCT: G. Chaverot, M. Turbet, H. Tran, J.-M. Hartmann, A. Campargue,
  D. Mondelain and E. Bolmont, Astron. Astrophys. 702 (2025) A137, DOI:
  10.1051/0004-6361/202555762, licensed under CC BY 4.0 (Tables 2 and 3, Eq. 11), as reduced
  values with their citation; CO2-N2's B2 alpha is taken as 0.00167, correcting the table's
  printed 0.0167 from the authors' own code (gitlab.com/ChaverotG/spect-public commit
  e7c4579c08, which states no licence: one number read as evidence, no code used). That set
  adapts M. Y. Perrin and J. M. Hartmann, J. Quant. Spectrosc. Radiat. Transfer 42 (1989)
  311-317, DOI: 10.1016/0022-4073(89)90077-0. The check against SpeCT's laboratory compilation
  (Zenodo, DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.15564294, CC BY 4.0) used it as data only. The line-by-line reference
  curves in apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/absorbers/earthLineReference.json,
  lineReferencesO2.json and lineReferencesH2O.json are computed from the same lines.

- Cross-sections of the pending molecules (1 nm cell means), derived from the HITRAN2024 line
  lists (I. E. Gordon et al., as above; https://hitran.org), with the stated overtone-ladder and
  band-model estimates, in apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/absorbers/bounds.json.

- Cross-sections of the pending absorbers, and NH3's and N2O's k-distributions, derived from the
  ExoMol database (J. Tennyson et al., the ExoMol database and the line lists each file names,
  https://www.exomol.com; J. Tennyson et al., J. Quant. Spectrosc. Radiat. Transf. 326 (2024)
  109083, DOI: 10.1016/j.jqsrt.2024.109083), licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0
  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/). The values in
  apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/absorbers/exomol/ are reduced from those line
  lists, a modification: bounds.json's cell means (plan R08, R08.T4.c); and (plan R08, R08.T4.g)
  the k-distributions in its kDistributions/ directory, the reference curves
  lineReferencesNH3.json and lineReferencesN2O.json, and n2oMerge.json, from the C2018 list of
  14NH3 (P. A. Coles, R. I. Ovsyannikov, O. L. Polyansky, S. N. Yurchenko and J. Tennyson,
  J. Quant. Spectrosc. Radiat. Transf. 219 (2018) 199-212, DOI: 10.1016/j.jqsrt.2018.07.022), with
  its H2 and He broadening files, and the TYM list of 14N2-16O (S. N. Yurchenko, T. M. Mellor and
  J. Tennyson, Mon. Not. R. Astron. Soc. 534 (2024) 1364-1375, DOI: 10.1093/mnras/stae2201), N2O's
  merged with HITRAN2024's bands (I. E. Gordon et al., as above). That adapted material is offered
  under CC BY-SA 4.0 (its README.md beside it); the line lists' files are not committed.

- Refractive indices of 75 and 84.5 wt% sulphuric acid at 300 K: K. F. Palmer and D. Williams,
  "Optical constants of sulfuric acid; application to the clouds of Venus?", Appl. Opt. 14
  (1975) 208-219, DOI: 10.1364/AO.14.000208, as distributed by the Aerosol Refractive Index
  Archive (ARIA), University of Oxford, https://eodg.atm.ox.ac.uk/ARIA/. Reduced to 360-780 nm
  in apps/hyperion/src/renderer/src/view/atmosphere/materials/sulphuric-acid-75.json and to the
  visible in sulphuric-acid-84.json. The 75 wt% values, with an added ultraviolet absorber whose
